  "mainEntity": {
    "name": "Why Your Shower Grout Is Cracking and When to Rebuild the Surround",
    "text": "Why Your Shower Grout Is Cracking and When to Rebuild the Surround",
    "@type": "Question",
    "answerCount": 1,
    "acceptedAnswer": {
      "text": "Question: Why is the grout in my shower corners cracking, and is it a sign I need professional reconstruction?\n\nAnswer: Cracking grout along your shower's inside corners, floor transitions, or curb joints is almost always caused by natural structural movement and using the wrong material during installation. Homes expand and contract with seasonal humidity and temperature shifts. When an installer packs rigid cementitious grout into these 90-degree plane changes, the joint cannot flex. It inevitably fractures, creating an open pathway for water to seep behind your tiles. \n\nHowever, persistent cracking across the flat face of your wall tiles or along the floor usually indicates a much deeper problem: excessive framing deflection, an unlevel substrate, or voids in the mortar bed underneath. If water has penetrated the backing material, the framing can swell, pushing tiles outward and loosening the grout.\n\nWhen Professional Guidance Helps:\nIf the issue is limited to clean corner joints, clearing out the cracked grout and applying a color-matched flexible sealant often solves the problem. But if you notice spongy drywall outside the shower enclosure, musty odors, loose tiles, or grout cracking across flat walls, the waterproof backing has likely failed. In that situation, rebuilding the shower surround with a proper ANSI-approved waterproofing membrane is necessary to stop hidden structural rot. HH Remodeling LLC handles bathroom tile, framing repairs, and complete tear-outs across the Denver metro area. Contact Abraham at 303-505-0866 for an on-site evaluation.",
      "@type": "Answer",
      "author": {
        "name": "HH Remodeling LLC",
        "@type": "Organization"
      }
    }
  }
